
[38;5;231m⏺[39m This is architectural, not bounded — I'll run the full process: questions, approaches, a sectioned design, then a
  written spec, before any code. Here's why, and it changes the first question:

  The repo is one static file — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m with an empty [38;5;153m<main>[39m and no JS, no styles, no build, no server, no
  dependencies. There is no task list, no storage, no concept of a user, and nothing that can "change" yet. So "notify
  users when tasks they care about change" currently has no tasks to watch, no users to notify, and no channel to notify
  them on.

  That means I need to know what actually exists before designing anything.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this repo is the whole app or just a shell over something real. It decides everything downstream — a
  notifications system with a backend is a fundamentally different build from one that lives entirely in a browser tab.

  - [1mBackend exists elsewhere[0m — I design against real task data and real user identity; email/push/multi-device are all
    on the table; the work is mostly integration.
  - [1mThis repo is all there is[0m — then tasks themselves don't exist yet, and notifications are a feature of an app nobody
    has built. We'd need to decompose: tasks first, notifications second. Cheap to decide now, expensive to discover
    halfway in.
  - [1mPrototype/demo only[0m — browser-local everything, no server, no real delivery. Fast, and deliberately throwaway.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m if this repo is genuinely all there is, we scope the task app first and treat notifications as the
  second sub-project — designing a notification system against tasks that don't exist means inventing their data model
  by accident, in the wrong spec.
